WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.264
Decision 1: what is a workflow, and what is agentic?

2
00:00:03.544 --> 00:00:09.581
Week 1 drew the line between workflows (code decides the steps) and agents (the model decides).

3
00:00:09.861 --> 00:00:19.888
Anthropic’s “Building effective agents” recommends the simplest solution that works, adding agentic complexity only when it demonstrably improves results.

4
00:00:20.168 --> 00:00:21.768
Apply that here, honestly.

5
00:00:22.048 --> 00:00:24.288
Much of this job is deterministic.

6
00:00:24.568 --> 00:00:28.131
Which issues changed this week is a query with a date filter.

7
00:00:28.411 --> 00:00:31.547
Which closed, which opened, which have no assignee: code.

8
00:00:31.827 --> 00:00:34.685
Where the report goes: fixed by configuration.

9
00:00:34.965 --> 00:00:49.152
The parts that benefit from a model are reading free-text bodies and comments to judge what actually happened (“fix is in review” versus “blocked on legal”), grouping related items, and writing a clear summary for a stakeholder.

10
00:00:49.432 --> 00:01:06.285
A defensible starting design is therefore a workflow with model-driven steps: code fetches and filters; a model turns each changed issue into a structured record; a model drafts the report from the records; code shows the draft to a reviewer and posts it on approval.

11
00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:08.251
Where would real agency help?

12
00:01:08.531 --> 00:01:15.784
Perhaps in deciding to read comments or linked issues when a body is ambiguous, which is a bounded, read-only loop.

13
00:01:16.064 --> 00:01:27.200
The rubric rewards a justified choice, in either direction: if you build a full agent loop, show on your eval that it beats the simpler design enough to pay for its extra cost and risk.
